{"id":"07-guest","audio_seconds":71.7651875,"script":{"id":"07-guest","title":"Demo 7 \u00b7 Fine-tuning: a guest who isn't on the class list","case":"A visitor speaks and is not enrolled: their lines must stay unknown and never be scored or credited to a student. Includes a claim the course slides don't cover.","lines":[{"speaker":"Prof Lim (demo)","text":"Today we have a visitor from industry sitting in. We're discussing parameter efficient fine tuning. Instead of updating all the weights, what does LoRA do?","start":0.8,"end":9.9},{"speaker":"Guest","text":"Hi everyone. In my team we use LoRA all the time. You freeze the base model and train two small low rank matrices that are added to the weight updates, so you only train a tiny fraction of the parameters.","start":10.93,"end":22.39},{"speaker":"Prof Lim (demo)","text":"Thank you. Students, why is that useful in practice?","start":23.12,"end":26.89},{"speaker":"Tasmin","text":"It needs much less memory, and you can store one small adapter per task instead of a full copy of the model.","start":27.77,"end":33.53},{"speaker":"Prof Lim (demo)","text":"Good. What's the rank in LoRA, intuitively?","start":34.83,"end":37.67},{"speaker":"Koh Qing Jia","text":"It's how many directions the update is allowed to change. A small rank like eight is often enough, and a larger rank gives more capacity but more parameters.","start":38.38,"end":50.43},{"speaker":"Guest","text":"And quantising the base model to four bits on top of that is what QLoRA does.","start":51.22,"end":55.43},{"speaker":"Tina","text":"I read that fine tuning with LoRA always beats retrieval augmented generation for factual questions.","start":56.49,"end":61.74},{"speaker":"Prof Lim (demo)","text":"I don't think the evidence supports always. For facts that change, retrieval is usually the better tool. Let's stop there.","start":62.76,"end":70.68}]},"registry":{"demo-professor":"Prof Demo","koh-qing-jia":"Koh Qing Jia","tasmin":"tasmin","tina":"tina","le-yong":"le yong","prof":"prof","demo-prof-lim":"Prof Lim (demo)"},"pipeline":{"language":"en","whisper_segments":[{"start":0.0,"end":10.0,"text":"Today we have a visitor from industry sitting in, where discussing parameter efficient fine tuning, instead of updating all the weights, what does Laura do?"},{"start":10.0,"end":22.0,"text":"Hi everyone, in my team we use Laura all the time, you freeze the base model and train two small low rank matrices that are added to the weight updates, so you only train a tiny fraction of the parameters."},{"start":22.0,"end":27.0,"text":"Thank you, students, why is that useful in practice?"},{"start":28.0,"end":34.0,"text":"It needs much less memory and you can store one small adapter per task instead of a full copy of the model."},{"start":34.0,"end":38.0,"text":"Good, what's the rank in Laura intuitively?"},{"start":38.0,"end":51.0,"text":"How many directions the update is allowed to change? A small rank like 8 is often enough and a larger rank gives more capacity but more parameters."},{"start":51.0,"end":56.0,"text":"And quantizing the base model to four bits on top of that is what QL or A does."},{"start":56.0,"end":62.0,"text":"I read that fine tuning with Laura always beats retrieval augmented generation for factual questions."},{"start":62.0,"end":71.0,"text":"I don't think the evidence supports always, for facts that change, retrieval is usually the better tool, let's stop there."}],"aligned_segments":[{"start":0.0,"end":10.0,"speaker":"SPEAKER_03","text":"Today we have a visitor from industry sitting in, where discussing parameter efficient fine tuning, instead of updating all the weights, what does Laura do?"},{"start":10.0,"end":22.0,"speaker":"SPEAKER_02","text":"Hi everyone, in my team we use Laura all the time, you freeze the base model and train two small low rank matrices that are added to the weight updates, so you only train a tiny fraction of the parameters."},{"start":22.0,"end":27.0,"speaker":"SPEAKER_03","text":"